{"title": "Insert mode & character handling: how Vaayu stays fast vs Helix and Neovim", "steps": [
{"file": "TECHNICAL_DECISIONS.md", "line": 184, "endLine": 191, "pattern": "| Operation | Vaayu | Compared with", "description": "Start with the evidence. In the 2026-10-03 benchmark (median key-to-first-output), inserting a character took 0.70 ms in Vaayu, 0.82 ms in bare Neovim and 406 ms in Helix on a 50k-line file. Leaving Insert took 0.50 ms in Vaayu and 51 ms in Neovim. The Neovim gap comes from its default 50 ms `ttimeoutlen` wait after Esc, so it is not an edit-speed difference. The doc itself warns that these runs don't isolate *why*. The rest of this tour walks through the code paths that plausibly explain the numbers: a rope buffer, a per-keystroke edit counter, incremental Tree-sitter parsing and highlight spans, a deferred parse on large files, and row-diffed rendering."},
{"file": "src/insert.rs", "line": 240, "endLine": 250, "pattern": "Key::Char(c) => {", "description": "Where a typed character lands. After the snippet, jk-escape and completion-popup checks earlier in `handle_inner`, a plain `Key::Char` does three things: an electric-dedent check for closing brackets, `insert_char`, and either an update or a close of the completion popup. No other work runs here, such as reparsing, re-highlighting or LSP sync. Those are left to `ensure_syntax` and the render loop, which decide later how much work is needed. That keeps the keystroke handler itself close to O(1)."},
{"file": "src/buffer.rs", "line": 920, "endLine": 929, "pattern": "pub fn insert_char(&mut self, line: usize, col: usize, ch: char)", "description": "The core edit is a single `ropey` insert. The (line, col) position is turned into a char index through the rope's line index (`char_idx`, about O(log n)), so no later bytes have to shift as they would in one big string. Neovim's memline and Helix's own rope use similar structures. The edit also bumps `edit_seq` and records a log entry. Line-shift bookkeeping (marks, folds) runs only when a '\\n' is inserted, so ordinary letters skip it. The comment above this block (around line 844) explains the design: undo batches the whole Insert session into one step, as in Vim, but `edit_seq` bumps on every keystroke so syntax/git/LSP never pair new text with a stale parse."},
{"file": "src/editor.rs", "line": 1040, "endLine": 1086, "pattern": "let key = (self.buf().id, self.buf().edit_seq);", "description": "`ensure_syntax` runs once per frame, not once per key, and does nothing unless `(buffer id, edit_seq)` changed. Its main Insert-mode optimization: on buffers of 256 KiB or more, it does not reparse while the user is typing. The text is drawn right away with the previous highlight spans, and the parse waits for a 150 ms quiet window (`INSERT_DEBOUNCE`). Leaving Insert gets one free frame (`defer_exit_frame`), so the switch to Normal mode is painted before the parse cost is paid. This is a likely reason large-file typing stays sub-millisecond where Helix measured hundreds of ms. Helix re-highlights synchronously on each edit."},
{"file": "src/editor.rs", "line": 763, "endLine": 773, "pattern": "pub(crate) fn buffer_text(&mut self)", "description": "Tree-sitter needs a contiguous `&str`, but the buffer is a rope. `buffer_text` builds that string once per `(buffer id, edit_seq)` and caches it as an `Rc<str>`, so syntax, LSP and other consumers in the same frame share one copy instead of each calling `rope.to_string()`. This full conversion is the O(n) step the large-buffer debounce in the previous step avoids while you type."},
{"file": "src/syntax.rs", "line": 546, "endLine": 600, "pattern": "pub fn reparse(&mut self, new_text: Rc<str>)", "description": "Incremental reparse. `reparse` works out what changed, applies it as an `InputEdit` to the old tree with `tree.edit`, and then calls `parser.parse(..., Some(old_tree))`, so Tree-sitter reuses every unaffected subtree. Next it tries `compute_incremental_spans`. If that bails out, it does a full span rebuild, but at most once every `FULL_REBUILD_THROTTLE`. `profile::note` timers around each phase (compute_edit / parse / rebuild_spans) are how the per-keystroke costs in BENCHMARKS.md were measured."},
{"file": "src/syntax.rs", "line": 778, "endLine": 803, "pattern": "fn compute_edit(old: &str, new: &str)", "description": "To produce the `InputEdit`, Vaayu compares the old and new text and finds their longest common prefix and suffix at the byte level. It does not track edit positions through every code path. This is a tight memcmp-style loop. For a one-character insert, the result is a minimal edit range that Tree-sitter can use to reuse almost the whole tree. Identical text returns `None`, which skips all further work."},
{"file": "src/syntax.rs", "line": 688, "endLine": 776, "pattern": "fn compute_incremental_spans(", "description": "This is the optimization that fixed the slow per-keystroke typing recorded in BENCHMARKS.md. Parsing was already incremental, but rebuilding the highlight-span list re-walked the whole tree from the root (~8 ms per keystroke; insert_char was ~8× slower than Neovim at one point). Now it takes `old_tree.changed_ranges(new_tree)`, widens that to the smallest enclosing node that can be classified, re-walks only that subtree, keeps the spans before it unchanged, and shifts the spans after it by the byte delta. The result is already sorted with no merge step, and it bails out (`None`) when the affected region covers more than half of a file larger than 4 KiB."},
{"file": "src/syntax.rs", "line": 360, "endLine": 363, "pattern": "/// Minimum gap between full-tree rebuilds", "description": "The fallback guard. Typing an unmatched `{` can make Tree-sitter's error recovery report that everything up to EOF changed, which forces a full span rebuild. `FULL_REBUILD_THROTTLE` (50 ms) limits those to one per window. A burst of keystrokes in broken-syntax territory therefore cannot pay the full walk on every key. Normal incremental updates are never throttled. For a short time highlighting may be stale, but it is always correct by the next window."},
{"file": "src/main.rs", "line": 263, "endLine": 303, "pattern": "loop {", "description": "Deferred work needs something to finish it. The idle loop blocks on `event::poll(IDLE_POLL_INTERVAL)`, so an idle editor does no redraw work. On each wakeup it checks `ed.syntax_catch_up_due()`, which covers both the 150 ms large-buffer debounce and the 50 ms rebuild throttle. When either is due, it runs `ensure_syntax()` and breaks out to redraw. An earlier version had a bug: highlighting stayed stale after a typing burst ended until the next key. This hook fixed it."},
{"file": "src/render.rs", "line": 1738, "endLine": 1760, "pattern": "for (y, row) in frame.iter().enumerate() {", "description": "Rendering is diffed by row. Each frame is composed in memory, and only rows whose bytes differ from `cache.rows` are sent to the terminal. Typing one character usually changes one text row plus the status line, so the output is a few hundred bytes rather than a full-screen repaint. Neovim's grid works the same way. Fewer bytes to the PTY lowers the key-to-first-output latency the benchmark measures."},
{"file": "src/insert.rs", "line": 134, "endLine": 134, "pattern": "Key::Esc => leave_insert(ed),", "description": "Last, the 0.5 ms vs 51 ms 'Leave Insert' result. crossterm decodes Esc into `Key::Esc` (src/key.rs:35), and Vaayu acts on it immediately with no `ttimeoutlen`-style wait for a possible escape sequence. That wait is exactly what bare Neovim spends by default. `leave_insert` then commits the batched undo transaction, records the change for `.`, and switches to Normal. Any deferred large-buffer parse runs on the following idle frame (see the earlier steps). The opt-in `jk_escape` path is the only Insert-mode key that waits on a timer (`timeoutlen_ms`)."}
]}
